Package: meshsat
Version: @VERSION@
Section: net
Priority: optional
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: MeshSat <hello@meshsat.net>
Installed-Size: @SIZE@
Depends: python3, python3-venv, python3-gi, gir1.2-gtk-4.0, gir1.2-adw-1, gir1.2-graphene-1.0, gir1.2-shumate-1.0, gir1.2-geoclue-2.0, python3-cryptography, python3-qrcode, systemd, adduser, openssl, curl, libc6@NODE_DEPENDS@
Recommends: libnotify-bin, bluez, gir1.2-gstreamer-1.0, gstreamer1.0-plugins-base, gstreamer1.0-plugins-good, gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad, gstreamer1.0-libcamera, gstreamer1.0-gtk4, gir1.2-edataserver-1.2, gir1.2-ebook-1.2, gir1.2-ebookcontacts-1.2, evolution-data-server
Homepage: https://meshsat.net
Description: MeshSat for Linux phones: the mesh, the satellite and the Hub in your pocket
 The MeshSat Bridge on this device, and the MeshSat app in the app grid with
 the same screens as MeshSat Android and iOS: a router between a Meshtastic
 mesh, a satellite modem and the MeshSat Hub. The app adopts a Meshtastic
 node over Bluetooth, as the other apps do; on a PinePhone with the Pine64
 LoRa back cover the package finds the cover by itself and runs the node on
 the phone (Meshtastic's Linux daemon with the MeshSat bridge layer for the
 cover's ATtiny I2C-to-SPI bridge), with a watchdog that tells the user when
 the radio stopped answering and the cover must be re-seated.
 .
 Pre-release. The radio in the cover is qualified at 0 dBm only (a plain
 crystal that drifts as the amplifier heats); range at that power is a
 house or a street. Built for Debian 13 (Mobian on arm64, desktops on amd64).
